    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d112">

                <pb facs="0208" n="126" sameAs="frus:frus1925v02/pg126"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890d.00/297: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Beirut</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Knabenshue</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-12-05T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 5,
                                1925—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>On basis of your recommendation the Department has arranged to have the Navy
                    Department withdraw the destroyers at once. The understanding is that they will
                    go to Alexandria. They are to stay there until December 15 so that the
                    Department may judge the effect of their departure before they proceed to the
                    western Mediterranean to join the <hi rendition="#italic">Pittsburgh.</hi></p>
                <p>Telegraph recommendations should you feel that it is important to have the
                    destroyers stay for a longer time near Beirut.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d112-f1"
                    /></p>
                <pb facs="0209" n="127" xml:id="pg127"/>
                <p>Separate instructions are being sent by Navy Department to captain of the
                    destroyers.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d112-f1" n="13" xml:id="d112-f1-content">
                <p>On the recommendation of the consul at Beirut, the two destroyers were retained
                    within a short cruising distance from Beirut. On December 28, the Navy
                    Department was informed that the consul had reported on December 26 that there
                    was no reason for keeping the destroyers longer in the vicinity of Beirut.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
